    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "The harp is among the oldest instruments — depicted in ancient Egypt and Mesopotamia. Sound from plucked strings; frame holds tension. Pedal harp (modern orchestra) and lever/folk harp (trad) serve different roles."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "irish-harp",
      "title": "Irish harp",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Wire-strung cláirseach historically; modern Irish harp (neo-Irish) nylon strings, levers for semitone changes. National symbol of Ireland. Turlough O'Carolan (1670–1738) composed harp music still central to trad repertoire."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "concert-harp",
      "title": "Concert harp",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "47 strings, seven pedals each altering pitch by semitone. Complex mechanism. Composers: Debussy (*Danse sacrée et danse profane*), Ravel."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "technique",
      "title": "Technique",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Pluck near middle of string for warmest tone. Harmonics, pres de la table (near soundboard) effects. Left hand bass, right hand melody in classical; both hands melodic in trad."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
